---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import Section from "../components/layout/Section.astro";
import Container from "../components/layout/Container.astro";
import Footer from "../components/layout/Footer.astro";
import ClassifiedBand from "../components/ui/ClassifiedBand.astro";
import PitchDeckNav from "../components/ui/PitchDeckNav.astro";
import CTA from "../components/ui/CTA.astro";
import Eyebrow from "../components/typography/Eyebrow.astro";
import Heading from "../components/typography/Heading.astro";
import Rule from "../components/typography/Rule.astro";
import "@/styles/global.css";
---

<Layout title="MI5 Executive Briefing — Cultural Soft-Power vs. Imperial Prestige">
  <ClassifiedBand label="TOP SECRET — MI5 EXECUTIVE BRIEFING // NARRATIVE PROTOCOL" />

  <!-- ─── PAGE HEADER ─── -->
  <Section variant="black" spacing="sm">
    <Container>
      <Eyebrow>Season Outline</Eyebrow>
      <Heading level={2} variant="section">The <em>Narrative Arc</em></Heading>
      <Rule />
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <!-- ─── VISUAL FORMATTING STRATEGY ─── -->
  <Section variant="paper" spacing="lg">
    <Container>
      <p class="prose">
        The narrative arc follows a split tonal timeline, visually representing the parallel worlds of the Crown/MI5 and the Beatles/Apple Corps, and their eventual intersection.
      </p>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <!-- ─── NARRATIVE TIMELINE ─── -->
  <div class="narrative-timeline">
    <!-- Central Tape Line -->
    <div class="tape-line">
      <div class="tape-label">MAGNETIC RECORDING TAPE — CLASSIFIED</div>
    </div>

    <!-- Upper Domain: The Crown & MI5 -->
    <div class="upper-domain">
      <div class="domain-label">UPPER DOMAIN — THE APPARATUS</div>
      
      <div class="episode-grid">
        <!-- Episode 1 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--mi5">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 1</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"A TOAST TO HER MAJESTY"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 1–16</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">October 26, 1965 – The MBE Ceremony at Buckingham Palace</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>In the royal bathroom, the working-class boys from Liverpool smoke a joint to calm their nerves. Minutes later, John Lennon delivers his casual "killing people" quip directly to the Crown, triggering a cold, quiet fury in Prince Philip. Viewing the band’s rising cultural authority as a direct threat to the establishment, Philip bypasses the Prime Minister and tasks two overlooked MI5 civil servants, Nigel Finch and Clive Barrow, with a classified containment project: <em>Operation Minuet</em>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">A crisp, symmetrical introduction to bureaucratic stagnation. Nigel just wants to protect his government pension; Clive thinks he has been handed a historical canvas.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 2 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--mi5">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 2</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"THE KLAUS TECHNIQUE"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 17–31</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">1966 – The <em>Rubber Soul</em> / <em>Revolver</em> sessions and the "More popular than Jesus" controversy</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>Tasked with manufacturing "subtle social wedges," the agents move into the field. Nigel manipulates George Harrison’s growing creative alienation by anonymously leaving a text on Eastern philosophy on his amp, inadvertently sparking George’s lifelong obsession with the sitar. Meanwhile, Clive adopts a ridiculous undercover persona—"Klaus," a pretentious German art critic—to infiltrate John's avant-garde circle. When John's "Jesus" quote hits the American press, Clive frantically works to amplify the Bible Belt backlash to ruin the band's US marketability.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">Fast-paced, institutional farce. The agents' clinical, paranoid surveillance logs directly contrast with the fluid, effortless creative explosion happening inside Abbey Road Studio Two.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 3 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--mi5">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 3</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"TACTICAL ADVANTAGES"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 32–49</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">August 1967 – The release of <em>Sgt. Pepper</em> and the sudden death of Brian Epstein</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The band achieves its counter-culture peak, leaving the agents feeling utterly defeated. However, the sudden, tragic overdose of the Beatles' immaculate manager, Brian Epstein, changes the board. In a chilling Buckingham Palace meeting, Prince Philip and Lord Mountbatten evaluate Epstein’s death not as a human tragedy, but as a "sudden, unexpected tactical advantage". With the band's primary protector and financial mediator gone, MI5 steps into the vacuum to accelerate the internal bleeding.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">The turning point. The series shifts focus, trading its lighter satirical comedy for a colder, psychological weight as the state apparatus begins to exploit the band's genuine grief and isolation.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 4 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--mi5">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 4</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"THE FREQUENCY OF REVOLUTION"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 50–60</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">1968 – The <em>White Album</em> sessions and the arrival of Yoko Ono</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>As the band fractures under the weight of their own egos, Nigel and Clive become trapped in a feedback loop of their own making. Listening to the grueling, fractured recording sessions through hidden MI5 microphones, Clive becomes profoundly unhinged. He stays up for days analyzing the avant-garde soundscape of "Revolution 9," entirely convinced it contains encrypted KGB launch codes and Soviet geographic coordinates. Nigel watches his partner lose his mind, realizing they are chasing ghosts while the band is simply destroying itself without their help.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">Claustrophobic, paranoid, and darkly funny. The agents begin to mirror the exact psychological deterioration they were ordered to inflict on their targets.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 5 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--mi5">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 5</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"OPERATION ELEGY"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 61–71</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">late 1969 – The miserable <em>Get Back</em> sessions and John returning his MBE</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>To widen the rift between the songwriters, Nigel intercepts a palace memo and sends Paul McCartney a forged letter hinting at an impending solo knighthood, making Paul appear like an establishment stooge to the radicalized John. The plan backfires spectacularly when John, furious at the establishment's continuous games, publicly returns his MBE medal to the Queen. This public rejection of a royal honor triggers total panic at the top. Prince Philip bypasses psychological containment entirely and demands a permanent solution: <em>Operation Elegy</em>—the authorized assassination of John Lennon during an upcoming public charity performance. Faced with actual blood on his hands, Nigel’s conscience finally breaks, turning him into a quiet saboteur of his own agency.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">High-stakes espionage thriller. The bleak, cold winter of 1969 mirrors Nigel’s moral crisis as he tries to dismantle a state-sponsored murder plot from inside his own cubicle.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 6 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--mi5">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 6</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"THE ULTIMATE COSMIC JOKE"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 72–87</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">1970 – The Rooftop Concert, the official breakup, and a 1980 Epilogue</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>Nigel successfully, quietly tanks the <em>Elegy</em> assassination attempt through orchestrated logistical errors and bad paperwork, forcing the state to retreat. The Beatles perform on the roof of Apple Corps and split forever, achieving the Crown's original goal through pure, organic creative exhaustion rather than covert design.</p>
            <p><strong>The Epilogue (1980):</strong> A jump forward in time. An aged, greyer Nigel and Clive sit at identical, dusty desks under flickering fluorescent bulbs in a forgotten, administrative outpost in the Falkland Islands—exiled by an embarrassed government. A small transistor radio crackles to life with a breaking news bulletin: <em>John Lennon has been shot and killed in New York by a deranged fan</em>.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">A crushing, brilliant dramatic irony. The agents stare at each other in the silence of their frozen office, realizing a lone madman achieved in a single afternoon what the entire multi-million dollar machinery of the British Empire spent five years failing to do. It ends on their faces as the wind howls outside—two forgotten old men who realize their secret war was fought for absolutely nothing.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Lower Domain: The Beatles & Apple Corps -->
    <div class="lower-domain">
      <div class="domain-label">LOWER DOMAIN — THE FREQUENCY</div>
      
      <div class="episode-grid">
        <!-- Episode 1 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--beatles">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 1</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"A TOAST TO HER MAJESTY"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 1–16</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">October 26, 1965 – The MBE Ceremony at Buckingham Palace</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The Beatles receive their MBE medals from the Queen. In the royal bathroom, the working-class boys from Liverpool smoke a joint to calm their nerves. John Lennon delivers his casual "killing people" quip directly to the Crown, setting in motion a chain of events that will echo through the coming decade.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">The beginning of the end. The band's innocent charm masks the cultural revolution they are about to unleash.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 2 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--beatles">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 2</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"THE KLAUS TECHNIQUE"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 17–31</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">1966 – The <em>Rubber Soul</em> / <em>Revolver</em> sessions and the "More popular than Jesus" controversy</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The band pushes musical boundaries with <em>Revolver</em> while John's "more popular than Jesus" comment sparks outrage in America. Their creative evolution creates both artistic breakthroughs and public relations disasters.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">Creative explosion meets cultural backlash. The band's confidence grows even as the establishment pushes back.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 3 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--beatles">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 3</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"TACTICAL ADVANTAGES"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 32–49</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">August 1967 – The release of <em>Sgt. Pepper</em> and the sudden death of Brian Epstein</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The band releases their masterpiece <em>Sgt. Pepper</em>, only to have their manager Brian Epstein die of an overdose. The loss creates a vacuum that will eventually tear them apart.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">Creative peak meets personal tragedy. The beginning of the end for the band's unity.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 4 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--beatles">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 4</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"THE FREQUENCY OF REVOLUTION"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 50–60</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">1968 – The <em>White Album</em> sessions and the arrival of Yoko Ono</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The band records the sprawling <em>White Album</em> as relationships fracture. John's relationship with Yoko Ono intensifies the tension within the group.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">Fracture and experimentation. The band's creative energy turns inward, destroying the unity that made them great.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 5 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--beatles">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 5</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"OPERATION ELEGY"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 61–71</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">late 1969 – The miserable <em>Get Back</em> sessions and John returning his MBE</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The band's tensions reach a breaking point during the <em>Get Back</em> sessions. John publicly returns his MBE in protest, signaling his complete rejection of the establishment.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">The final spiral. The band's internal conflicts reach a climax as the world around them descends into chaos.</div>
        </div>

        <!-- Episode 6 -->
        <div class="episode-card episode-card--beatles">
          <div class="episode-number">EPISODE 6</div>
          <div class="episode-title">"THE ULTIMATE COSMIC JOKE"</div>
          <div class="episode-scenes">Scenes 72–87</div>
          <div class="episode-milestone">1970 – The Rooftop Concert, the official breakup, and a 1980 Epilogue</div>
          <div class="episode-narrative">
            <p>The band performs their famous rooftop concert and officially breaks up. Years later, John Lennon is assassinated by Mark David Chapman, completing the tragic circle.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="episode-tone">Ironic tragedy. The cultural revolution they started consumes one of its leaders, while the spies who tried to stop them end up as forgotten relics.</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Connection Lines (CSS only) -->
    <div class="connection-lines">
      <!-- These will be created with CSS absolutely positioned -->
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- ─── NAVIGATION ─── -->
  <PitchDeckNav current="/narrative" />

  <!-- ─── FOOTER ─── -->
  <Footer />
</Layout>

<style>
  /* ─── NARRATIVE TIMELINE LAYOUT ─── */
  .narrative-timeline {
    position: relative;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 150vh; /* Will expand based on content */
    background: var(--bg);
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* ─── CENTRAL TAPE LINE ─── */
  .tape-line {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: linear-gradient(to right, 
      transparent 0%, 
      var(--accent-blue) 10%, 
      var(--accent-blue) 90%, 
      transparent 100%);
    transform: translateY(-50%);
    z-index: 10;
  }

  .tape-line::before,
  .tape-line::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    width: 20px;
    height: 20px;
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    border-radius: 50%;
    transform: translateY(-50%);
  }

  .tape-line::before {
    left: 10%;
  }

  .tape-line::after {
    right: 10%;
  }

  .tape-label {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    background: var(--bg);
    padding: 4px 12px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
  }

  /* ─── DOMAINS ─── */
  .upper-domain {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 50%;
    background: #e0e0e0; /* Sterile grey */
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--accent-blue);
    overflow-y: auto;
    overflow-x: hidden;
  }

  .lower-domain {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    bottom: 0;
    background: #f5f0e1; /* Warm cream */
    border-top: 2px solid var(--accent-blue);
    overflow-y: auto;
    overflow-x: hidden;
  }

  .domain-label {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    left: 0;
    padding: 8px 16px;
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.6rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    background: var(--bg);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-right: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    color: var(--text-muted);
    z-index: 5;
  }

  .upper-domain .domain-label {
    top: auto;
    bottom: 0;
    left: auto;
    right: 0;
    border: none;
    border-left: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
  }

  /* ─── EPISODE GRID ─── */
  .episode-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fill, minmax(300px, 1fr));
    gap: var(--space-6);
    padding: var(--space-8) var(--space-6);
    max-width: var(--container-width);
    margin: 0 auto;
    width: 100%;
  }

  /* ─── EPISODE CARDS ─── */
  .episode-card {
    background: var(--bg);
    border: 1px solid var(--border-faint);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-5);
    transition: transform 0.2s ease, box-shadow 0.2s ease;
    position: relative;
  }

  .episode-card:hover {
    transform: translateY(-4px);
    box-shadow: 0 12px 24px -8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.12);
    border-color: var(--accent-blue);
  }

  .episode-card--mi5 {
    background: #fff;
    border-color: #e0e0e0;
  }

  .episode-card--beatles {
    background: #fff;
    border-color: #e0e0e0;
  }

  .episode-number {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.7rem;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: 0.1rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--accent-blue);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
  }

  .episode-title {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    color: var(--text1);
  }

  .episode-scenes {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
  }

  .episode-milestone {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.95rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    line-height: 1.5;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-3);
    color: var(--text3);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--accent-blue);
    padding-left: var(--space-3);
  }

  .episode-narrative {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-4);
  }

  .episode-narrative p {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--text2);
  }

  .episode-tone {
    margin-top: var(--space-4);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border-soft);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 500;
    letter-spacing: 0.05rem;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--text-muted);
  }

  /* ─── CONNECTION LINES (CSS ONLY) ─── */
  .connection-lines {
    position: absolute;
    top: 50%;
    left: 0;
    right: 0;
    height: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  .connection-line {
    position: absolute;
    top: 0;
    height: 2px;
    background: var(--accent-blue);
    opacity: 0.6;
    transform-origin: left center;
  }

  /* ─── RESPONSIVE ─── */
  @media (max-width: 800px) {
    .narrative-timeline {
      min-height: 200vh;
    }

    .episode-grid {
      padding: var(--space-6) var(--space-4);
    }

    .episode-card {
      padding: var(--space-4);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .episode-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    .tape-label {
      font-size: 0.6rem;
      padding: 3px 10px;
    }
  }
</style>